[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 968,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 733,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139543,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 733,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Myopericytoma is a perivascular myoid neoplasm on a continuum with myofibroma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 969,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 734,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139544,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 734,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8824/0 Myofibroma</p>\r\n<p>8824/1 Multicentric myofibromatosis</p>\r\n<p>8824/0 Myopericytoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 970,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 735,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139545,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 735,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F36.Y &amp; XH2HE9 Other specified benign neoplasm of eye or ocular adnexa &amp; Myopericytoma</p>\r\n<p>2F36.Y &amp; XH0953 Other specified benign neoplasm of eye or ocular adnexa &amp; Myofibroma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 971,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 736,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139546,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 736,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 972,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 737,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139547,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 737,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Myofibroma; myofibromatosis; infantile myofibromatosis</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 973,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 738,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139548,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 738,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ophthalmic myopericytoma usually arises in the dermis or subcutis of the eyelid {33089011}, and rarely the orbit {25931311;33089011;33632050}. Paediatric myofibroma may involve orbital or periorbital sites, with frequent involvement of periorbital bones and the skull base, particularly in the setting of infantile myofibromatosis {3538883;3207313;8573030;9307643;9823360;16855503;21512784}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 974,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 739,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139549,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 739,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours usually occur as a painless mass, sometimes with ptosis or eye displacement {21512784}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 975,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 740,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139550,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 740,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Solitary cases predominate in adults, with a male predominance {16330949}; multifocal myofibromatosis arises in paediatric patients, most often at birth or in the first 2&nbsp;years of life, and has a female predominance {7284977;8573030}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 976,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 741,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139551,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 741,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Infantile myofibromatosis is familial, with reports of both autosomal dominant {12894106;3236168;21834820} and recessive {11260217} inheritance.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 977,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 742,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139552,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 742,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>PDGFRB</em> mutations are common {28505006;27776010}, with germline mutations seen in familial cases {23731537;23731542}. Cellular myofibromas may demonstrate recurrent <em>SRF </em>rearrangement {28248815;31478943;33021523}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 984,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 749,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139553,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 749,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Myopericytomas are usually circumscribed, firm, and homogeneous. Orbital cases of infantile myofibroma average 30&nbsp;mm (range: 2&ndash;100&nbsp;mm) with frequent bone destruction {21512784}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 978,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 743,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139554,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 743,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Myopericytomas are unencapsulated, circumscribed, variably cellular, nodular lesions composed of myoid spindle cells concentrically arranged around prominent vasculature {16330949}. Myofibromas are similar but often demonstrate a biphasic pattern, with central hypercellularity showing plump immature-appearing spindle cells, and peripheral nodular growth showing variable hyalinization {21512784}. &ldquo;Cellular myofibroma/myopericytoma&rdquo; refers to hypercellular and hyperproliferative lesions with infiltrative growth but lacking frankly malignant features including pleomorphism or necrosis {28248815}; these have not been described in ophthalmic cases.</p>\r\n<p>Myopericytomas/myofibromas express SMA and are often negative for desmin (except <em>SRF-</em>rearranged lesions). H-caldesmon may be expressed by both, although less consistently in myofibromas.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 979,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 744,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139555,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 744,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cytology is not reported for ophthalmic cases.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 980,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 745,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139556,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 745,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>PDGFRB </em>point mutations or <em>SRF</em> rearrangement can be diagnostically supportive in the appropriate context.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 983,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 748,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139557,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 748,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> for myopericytoma: bland myoid spindle cells with perivascular concentric growth; for myofibroma: biphasic pattern with immature hypercellular areas surrounded by a less cellular myoid population.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable (in select cases):</em> SMA immunopositivity; <em>PDGFRB</em> point mutations or <em>SRF</em> rearrangements.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 981,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 746,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139558,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 746,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 982,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 747,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139559,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 747,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Myopericytomas are clinically indolent, although the rare <em>SRF</em>-rearranged tumours can behave aggressively {31478943}. Solitary myofibromas likewise have an excellent prognosis, with frequent spontaneous regression; occasionally they recur locally after incomplete excision. Cases of multicentric myofibromatosis, particularly those with visceral involvement, have a significantly worse outcome {21512784}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]